  "preamble": "Replacement of the glottal fricative [h] by the voiced velar stop [g] is known mainly from loanword adaptation in Russian, which has no /h/. Until about the mid-20th century, foreign [h] in words of German, English, Latin or Greek origin was conventionally written with Cyrillic г and pronounced [g] in the standard language (Гамбург 'Hamburg', Гитлер 'Hitler', галстук < German Halstuch). The convention is linked to the use of г for [ɦ] or [ɣ] in Ukrainian, Belarusian and southern Russian dialects, but in standard Russian the result is a plain stop. More recent borrowings use х [x] instead (хобби 'hobby').",
